<div class="article-body">

<p>My first week with hydroponic nutrients, I killed 3 lettuce seedlings. I mixed the solution too strong. The leaves turned brown at the edges and curled up. I thought it was a disease. I added more nutrients. They died faster.</p>

<p>If you're <a href="/posts/grow-lettuce-indoors-year-round">growing lettuce indoors</a>, nutrients are the most intimidating part. Here's the short version: you only need one nutrient line to start.</p>

<p>Nutrients are the most intimidating part of hydroponics. There are 3 bottles, 5 bottles, 10 bottles. There are A and B parts. There are bloom boosters and root stimulators. It's overwhelming.</p>

<p>But here's the truth: you only need one nutrient line to start. And mixing it takes 2 minutes. This guide covers exactly what to buy and how to use it.</p>

<h2 id="why">1. Why nutrients confuse everyone</h2>

<p>Plants need 16 different elements to grow. In soil, most of these come from the dirt. In hydroponics, you have to supply all of them in the water. That's why you need a complete nutrient solution, not just fertilizer.</p>

<p>The good news is that nutrient companies have already figured this out. You don't need to buy 16 separate bottles. You buy a 2-part or 3-part system and mix them. That's it.</p>

<p>The confusing part is that every brand uses different ratios. Some are for lettuce, some for tomatoes, some for "general purpose." For a beginner, just get a general purpose formula. It works for everything.</p>

<h2 id="what">2. What to buy: the 3 best nutrient lines</h2>

<p>I've used all three of these. They all work. Pick one and stick with it.</p>

<p><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=General+Hydroponics+FloraSeries+nutrient+kit&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">General Hydroponics FloraSeries</a>.</strong> This is the most popular nutrient line in the world. It comes in 3 bottles: FloraMicro, FloraGro, and FloraBloom. You mix them in different ratios depending on the plant stage. For lettuce and herbs, use the "light vegetative" ratio. For tomatoes, use the "bloom" ratio. A $35 starter kit lasts 3 months for a 4 plant setup. This is what I use and recommend.</p>

<p><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Botanicare+Pure+Blend+Pro&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Botanicare Pure Blend Pro</a>.</strong> This is a 2-part system. Simpler than FloraSeries but slightly more expensive. It's made from organic ingredients, which some people prefer. Works well for lettuce and herbs. About $40 for a set that lasts 2 months.</p>

<p><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=MasterBlend+4-18-38+hydroponic+fertilizer&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">MasterBlend 4-18-38</a>.</strong> This is the budget option. It's a powder, not a liquid. You mix it with water and add calcium nitrate and magnesium sulfate (Epsom salt). It's cheap ($15 for a huge bag) but requires more measuring. Popular with serious growers who want to save money.</p>

<p>If you're a beginner, get the <strong>General Hydroponics FloraSeries</strong>. It's the most forgiving and the easiest to find.</p>

<h2 id="how">3. How to mix nutrients</h2>

<p>Here's the exact process I use. It takes 2 minutes.</p>

<p>Step 1. Fill your reservoir with water. Use a 5 gallon bucket for a standard DWC system.</p>

<p>Step 2. Add the nutrients in the right order. For FloraSeries: add FloraMicro first, stir, then FloraGro, stir, then FloraBloom, stir. Do NOT mix them all together before adding to water. They can react and form solids that plants can't absorb.</p>

<p>Step 3. Start with half the recommended dose. If the bottle says 5ml per gallon, use 2.5ml. Wait 3 days. If your plants look good, increase to 3/4 dose. If they look great, go to full dose. This is called "ramping up." It prevents nutrient burn.</p>

<p>Step 4. Check the pH (see next section).</p>

<p>Step 5. Change the water completely every 2 weeks. Don't just top it off. Old water builds up salts that mess with the nutrient balance.</p>

<h2 id="ph">4. The pH problem</h2>

<p>pH is the single most common cause of sick plants in hydroponics. Your plants could have perfect nutrients and perfect light, but if the pH is wrong, they can't absorb anything.</p>

<p>The target pH for hydroponics is 5.5 to 6.5. Most tap water is around 7.0 to 8.0. You need to bring it down.</p>

<p><strong>What to buy.</strong> Get a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pH+test+kit+liquid+drops+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">pH test kit with liquid drops</a> ($8) or a digital pH meter ($15). The liquid drops are more reliable. Digital meters need calibration and the cheap ones drift over time.</p>

<p><strong>How to fix it.</strong> Buy <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=General+Hydroponics+pH+Down+solution&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">pH Down solution</a> ($10). Add a few drops at a time, stir, and test. Repeat until you hit 5.8 to 6.0. The pH will drift up over a few days as the plants absorb nutrients. Check it every 3 days and adjust.</p>

<p>I ignored pH for my first 2 weeks. My plants were pale and sad. I checked the pH and it was 7.8. I added pH Down to 5.8. Within 24 hours, the leaves were darker green. It's that dramatic.</p>

<h2 id="problems">5. Three signs your plants are starving</h2>

<p><strong>Yellow lower leaves.</strong> The plant is moving nitrogen from old leaves to new growth. Add more nitrogen (increase the FloraGro ratio).</p>

<p><strong>Brown spots on leaves.</strong> Calcium deficiency. This happens when pH is too low or when there's not enough calcium in the water. Check pH first, then add CalMag supplement if needed.</p>

<p><strong>Purple stems.</strong> Phosphorus deficiency. Usually caused by cold water (below 60F) rather than actual lack of phosphorus. Warm up your reservoir.</p>

<p>Most nutrient problems are actually pH problems. Before you change anything, check your pH. 9 times out of 10, that's the issue.</p>

